      "passage": "<p>(1) Cities are often pictured as seas of concrete, glass and traffic. Yet in recent years, planners around the world have rediscovered an old idea: that people live better when nature is close at hand. Parks, community gardens, tree-lined streets and even green roofs are now seen as essential parts of a modern city rather than luxuries added when money is left over.</p><p>(2) The benefits are well documented. Trees absorb dust and carbon dioxide, and their shade can lower the temperature of a street by several degrees during a heatwave. Grass and soil soak up rainwater, which reduces the risk of flooding after heavy storms. Green spaces also provide a home for birds, bees and other wildlife that would otherwise disappear from built-up areas.</p><p>(3) Perhaps less obvious is the effect on people's minds. Research in several countries suggests that residents who live within a short walk of a park report lower stress and better mood than those who do not. Parks also give neighbours a place to meet, play sport or simply sit, which strengthens the sense of community. For children in apartment blocks, a playground may be the only place where they can run freely.</p><p>(4) Creating green space in a crowded city is, however, far from easy. Land is expensive, and developers usually prefer to build offices or flats that bring a quick profit. Some cities have found creative answers. Seoul, for example, removed a motorway and replaced it with a stream and a public walkway, while Singapore encourages many new buildings to include gardens on their walls or roofs. Such projects show that even limited space can be turned into something living.</p><p>(5) Critics argue that parks are costly to maintain and that the money could be better spent on public transport or housing. This view overlooks the fact that green spaces save money in the long run, by cutting health costs and cooling buildings. The real challenge, then, is not whether cities can afford to be green but whether they can afford not to be.</p>",
