      "id": "ta6-17",
      "part": "ta",
      "passage": "<p>(1) When Hana, aged sixteen, first agreed to spend her Saturday mornings at a local food bank, she expected a tiring chore that would look good on her university application. Six months later, she is still there, and she no longer thinks about the application at all. \"I came to give something,\" she says, \"but I seem to have received far more.\"</p><p>(2) Hana's experience is far from unusual. Surveys of young volunteers in several countries show that most begin for practical reasons: a school requirement, a wish to build a CV, or pressure from friends. Yet a large majority say that they continued because they enjoyed the work and felt that it mattered. Volunteering, it seems, often starts as an obligation and turns into a choice.</p><p>(3) The advantages for young people are considerable. Volunteers gain skills that classrooms rarely teach: organising events, working with strangers, and staying calm under pressure. They also meet people of different ages and backgrounds, which can challenge assumptions they did not know they had. Employers increasingly notice this; many say that they would choose a candidate with volunteering experience over one with slightly higher grades but no practical background.</p><p>(4) Nevertheless, volunteering is not without problems. Some charities report that students who sign up only to collect certificates leave as soon as they have enough hours, which wastes the time spent training them. Others warn that unpaid work should never replace paid jobs. If organisations rely too heavily on volunteers, they may avoid hiring the professionals who are needed.</p><p>(5) The solution is not to discourage young people from volunteering but to improve how it is organised. Schools can let students choose causes that genuinely interest them rather than assigning placements, and charities can give volunteers real responsibility instead of simple, repetitive tasks. When young people feel trusted, they are far more likely to stay. Done well, volunteering benefits not only those who receive help but also those who give it.</p>",
